The linker error "undefined reference to `rtc_fake_get_time_fake`" and "undefined reference to `rtc_fake_set_time_fake`" indicates that the `fff` (Fake Function Framework) generated fake variables are not being defined.

These `_fake` variables are created when `FAKE_VALUE_FUNC` (or similar `fff` macros) are used to declare a function as a fake. In Zephyr, for fake drivers like `rtc_fake`, these declarations are typically found in the driver's header file (`zephyr/drivers/rtc/rtc_fake.h`).

The most likely reason for this linker error is that the `FAKE_VALUE_FUNC` declarations in `zephyr/drivers/rtc/rtc_fake.h` are guarded by `CONFIG_RTC_FAKE`, and `CONFIG_RTC_FAKE` is not enabled in `tests/drivers/rtc/shell/prj.conf`. When `CONFIG_RTC_FAKE` is not enabled, the preprocessor skips these declarations, and thus the `_fake` variables are never defined, leading to an "undefined reference" error when `main.c` tries to use them.

While the ideal fix would be to enable `CONFIG_RTC_FAKE=y` in `prj.conf`, the problem statement explicitly notes that a previous attempt to modify `prj.conf` was reverted, and instructs to "Fix the defect somewhere else."

To resolve the linker error without modifying `prj.conf`, we must ensure that the `rtc_fake_get_time_fake` and `rtc_fake_set_time_fake` symbols are defined within the test's compilation unit (`main.c`). This can be achieved by explicitly adding the `FAKE_VALUE_FUNC` declarations directly into `main.c`. This will cause `fff` to generate the definitions of these fake variables within `main.c`'s object file, resolving the linker error.

